  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**What the supply\u2011graph tells us**\n\nUsing **traverse_supply_graph (downstream, max\u202ftier\u202f=\u202f2)** on *Xiamen Tungsten* produced a list of 105 \u201cenriched\u201d downstream entities \u2013 i.e., companies that either buy tungsten products from Xiamen Tungsten or could use it as an alternative source.  \n\n- **Tier\u20111 (direct customers / immediate alternatives)** \u2013 12 firms that are most tightly linked to the tungsten supplier, for example:  \n  -\u202fCALB (China) \u2013 EV lithium\u2011ion battery cell maker  \n  -\u202fTDK (Japan) \u2013 passive electronic components  \n  -\u202fGotion High\u2011Tech (China) \u2013 LFP battery cells  \n  -\u202fMurata Manufacturing (Japan) \u2013 MLCCs  \n  -\u202fContemporary Amperex Technology (CATL, China) \u2013 EV battery cells  \n  -\u202fSamsung SDI (South Korea) \u2013 lithium\u2011ion cells  \n  -\u202fBYD (China) \u2013 BEVs & batteries  \n  -\u202fSumitomo Electric Industries (Japan) \u2013 wiring harnesses  \n  -\u202fZhejiang Founder Motor (China) \u2013 EV drive motors  \n  -\u202fSunwoda Electronic (China) \u2013 battery packs & cells  \n  -\u202fPanasonic (Japan) \u2013 automotive lithium\u2011ion cells  \n  -\u202fHunan Corun New Energy (China) \u2013 Ni\u2011MH cells  \n\n- **Tier\u20112 (customers of Tier\u20111 firms, or downstream vehicle manufacturers)** \u2013 a long list that includes the world\u2019s major auto makers and EV players such as **Tesla, BYD, NIO, XPeng, Li Auto, Rivian, Volkswagen, General Motors, Ford, Mercedes\u2011Benz, Hyundai, Kia, SAIC Motor, Geely, Renault, Volvo AB**, plus many component suppliers (e.g., **TDK, Murata, Sumitomo Electric, Magna International, Aisin, ZF Friedrichshafen**).\n\nThese companies are *structurally* at risk because a disruption at Xiamen Tungsten could reduce the supply of tungsten\u2011based products (e.g., high\u2011performance alloys, hard metal tools, specialty powders) that they either purchase directly or rely on through their own tier\u20111 suppliers.\n\n---\n\n**Evidence gathered to confirm the link**\n\n| Company | Evidence source(s) consulted | What was found |\n|---------|-----------------------------|----------------|\n| **BYD** (representative Tier\u20111) | `get_supplier_info` (filing request for BYD, 2010\u201109\u201122) | No filing on record \u2013\u202fthe system returned \u201cNo filings on record for \u2018BYD Company Ltd\u2019\u201d. |\n| **Xiamen Tungsten** (the disrupted firm) | `search_corpus` for the exact phrase \u201cXiamen Tungsten\u201d (date\u202f=\u202f2010\u201109\u201122) | Returned only unrelated 2004 Panasonic and 2009/2010 Aluminum Corp of China filings; none mention Xiamen Tungsten or any downstream relationship. |\n| **Other Tier\u20111/Tier\u20112 firms** (e.g., CALB, TDK, CATL, Samsung\u202fSDI, Murata, etc.) | No targeted `get_supplier_info` calls were left \u2013 the remaining two tool calls were already spent on the generic \u201cXiamen Tungsten\u201d search. Consequently no company\u2011specific filing or corpus mention was retrieved for any of them. |\n\n---\n\n**What could not be confirmed**\n\n* **Direct documentary evidence that any of the listed downstream companies actually source tungsten (or tungsten\u2011based components) from Xiamen\u202fTungsten.**  \n  *The supply\u2011graph algorithm infers a connection based on industry\u2011wide material flows, but none of the filings or corpus excerpts we examined mention Xiamen\u202fTungsten by name.*\n\n* **Any public disclosure that the September\u202f2010 disruption materially affected production, deliveries, or financial results of the downstream firms.**  \n  *Searches of SEC\u2011style filings around the event date returned only unrelated documents (Panasonic 2004 annual report, Aluminum Corp of China agreements) with no reference to Xiamen\u202fTungsten.*\n\n* **Specific impact on Tier\u20112 vehicle manufacturers (Tesla, Volkswagen, GM, etc.).**  \n  *Because we exhausted our four\u2011call budget on the initial graph and two generic searches, we were unable to pull individual filings for these companies that might mention tungsten supply constraints.*\n\n---\n\n### Bottom line\n\n- The **structural analysis** identifies a large set of downstream firms\u2014ranging from battery cell producers (CALB, CATL, Samsung\u202fSDI) through component makers (TDK, Murata, Sumitomo Electric) to major automotive OEMs (Tesla, BYD, Volkswagen, GM, etc.)\u2014that could be **theoretically exposed** to a supply interruption at Xiamen\u202fTungsten.\n\n- **No concrete filing\u2011level or corpus evidence** was found that any of these companies publicly acknowledged reliance on Xiamen\u202fTungsten, nor that they reported operational or financial effects from the September\u202f2010 disruption.\n\n- Consequently, while the *potential* scope of impact is broad (as shown by the supply\u2011graph), **the actual material impact remains unverified** with the data available within our four\u2011call limit. Further investigation would require targeted retrieval of individual company filings (e.g., 10\u2011K/20\u2011F risk factor sections, MD&A commentary) and possibly news\u2011wire or press\u2011release searches beyond the SEC filing corpus.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (104) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - calb: no evidence gathered\n  - tdk: no evidence gathered\n  - gotion high-tech: no evidence gathered\n  - murata manufacturing: no evidence gathered\n  - contemporary amperex technology: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung sdi: no evidence gathered\n  - byd: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo electric industries: no evidence gathered\n  - zhejiang founder motor: no evidence gathered\n  - sunwoda electronic: no evidence gathered\n  - hunan corun new energy: no evidence gathered\n  - guangzhou automobile: no evidence gathered\n  - great wall motor: no evidence gathered\n  - obayashi: no evid